\title{Height Reduction for Cyclotomic Polynomials: the Odd Radical, the Flat Class, and the Ternary Trichotomy}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{22 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
Let $\Phi_n \in \mathbb{Z}[X]$ be the $n$-th cyclotomic polynomial and let its \emph{height} $H(n)$ be the largest absolute value of its coefficients. We develop a complete reduction theory for $H$ from two elementary symmetries --- \emph{inflation} $\Phi_{np}(X)=\Phi_n(X^p)$ for $p \mid n$, and \emph{reflection} $\Phi_{2n}(X)=\Phi_n(-X)$ for odd $n>1$ --- and prove the \emph{height reduction theorem}: for every $n \ge 1$ and every $B$, all coefficients of $\Phi_n$ are bounded by $B$ in absolute value if and only if the same holds for $\Phi_{\radodd(n)}$, where $\radodd(n)$ is the product of the odd primes dividing $n$. Hence $H(n) = H(\radodd(n))$: the height is blind to the prime $2$ and to repeated prime factors. Coupled with the two-parameter (Migotti) theorem this yields the \emph{flatness classification}: $\Phi_n$ has all coefficients in $\{-1,0,1\}$ whenever the odd part of $n$ has at most two distinct prime divisors, and the bound is attained. Since $105=3\cdot5\cdot7$ is the least integer with three distinct odd prime divisors, this explains why $\Phi_n$ is flat for every $n<105$. We then determine three ternary cyclotomic polynomials exactly, by cancellation in the associated M\"obius identity: $\Phi_{231}$ (degree $120$) is flat; $\Phi_{105}$ (degree $48$) has height exactly $2$, attained at $X^7$ and $X^{41}$; $\Phi_{385}$ (degree $240$) has height exactly $3$, attained at $X^{119},X^{120},X^{121}$. Height reduction propagates each value to the whole odd-radical class, giving three infinite families of heights $1$, $2$, $3$, all with exactly three odd prime divisors. Thus $\oddw$ does not determine the height while $\radodd$ does, and the flatness classification is a strict implication. Finally, height $1$ is exactly flatness, and every coefficient of the flat two-prime-power families is an explicit difference of indicator functions of the numerical semigroup $\langle p,q\rangle$.
\end{abstract}

